const GENGRAMMAR = [{"id": "gg:agreement-01", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-01", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>The number of commuters who ride bicycles across the city’s bridges have nearly doubled over the past decade.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>commuters</i> to <i>commuter</i>.", "Change <i>have</i> to <i>has</i>.", "Change <i>ride</i> to <i>rides</i>.", "Change <i>doubled</i> to <i>double</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects the sentence. The subject of the main verb is the singular noun <i>number</i>, so the verb must be <i>has</i>, not <i>have</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> <i>Commuters</i> must stay plural: <i>the number of</i> is followed by a plural noun, and the plural verb <i>ride</i> depends on it.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> <i>The number of</i> always takes a singular verb. The plural nouns <i>commuters</i>, <i>bicycles</i>, and <i>bridges</i> stand between the subject and the verb, but the subject is <i>number</i>: <i>The number … has nearly doubled</i>.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> <i>Who</i> refers to the plural <i>commuters</i>, so <i>ride</i> is already correct.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> After the helping verb the past participle <i>doubled</i> is required; <i>have double</i> is not a verb form.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-02", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-02", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) A coral reef, though it resembles a mass of colorful rocks, is built by colonies of tiny animals called polyps. (2) Each of the polyps secretes a hard limestone skeleton that remains long after the animal has died. (3) Living among the branches of the coral are thousands of fish, crabs, and sponges that depend on the reef for shelter. (4) Neither rising water temperatures nor pollution from coastal cities have been easy for these fragile ecosystems to withstand.</blockquote><p>Which sentence contains an error in subject-verb agreement?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence has a verb that does not agree with its subject. In sentence 4 the subjects are joined by <i>neither … nor</i>, so the verb agrees with the nearer subject, the singular <i>pollution</i>; <i>have been</i> should be <i>has been</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1 the singular subject <i>reef</i> correctly takes <i>is built</i>, even though a clause separates them.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2 the subject is the singular <i>Each</i>, not <i>polyps</i>, so <i>secretes</i> is correct.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 3 the subject follows the verb: <i>thousands of fish, crabs, and sponges</i> is plural, so <i>are</i> is correct, and <i>depend</i> agrees with the same plural group.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> With <i>neither … nor</i> the verb agrees with the subject closer to it. That subject is <i>pollution</i>; the plural <i>cities</i> is only part of the phrase <i>from coastal cities</i>. The sentence should read <i>Neither rising water temperatures nor pollution from coastal cities has been easy</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-03", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-03", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883 its two stone towers, each of which rises more than 270 feet above the river, was taller than nearly every building in New York. (2) John Roebling, the engineer who designed the bridge, died before construction began; his son Washington took over the project. (3) After Washington fell ill, his wife, Emily Roebling, carried his instructions to the workers and oversaw much of the remaining construction. (4) Tens of thousands of vehicles, as well as thousands of pedestrians and cyclists, now cross the bridge each day.</blockquote><p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Insert a comma after <i>1883</i> AND change <i>was</i> to <i>were</i>.", "Sentence 2: Change <i>died</i> to <i>dies</i> AND change the semicolon to a comma.", "Sentence 3: Delete the comma after <i>ill</i> AND change <i>oversaw</i> to <i>oversees</i>.", "Sentence 4: Change <i>cross</i> to <i>crosses</i> AND delete the comma after <i>cyclists</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence contains two errors that the paired revisions would fix. Sentence 1 needs a comma after the introductory clause <i>When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883</i>, and its plural subject <i>towers</i> needs the plural verb <i>were</i>.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> The opening dependent clause must be set off with a comma after <i>1883</i>. The subject of the main clause is <i>towers</i>; the clause <i>each of which rises more than 270 feet above the river</i> has its own singular subject and verb, but it does not change the main subject, so <i>was</i> must become <i>were</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> <i>Died</i> correctly matches the past-tense verbs around it, and the semicolon correctly joins two complete sentences; a comma there would create a run-on.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The comma after <i>ill</i> correctly follows an introductory clause, and <i>oversaw</i> correctly matches the past-tense <i>carried</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The subject is the plural <i>Tens of thousands of vehicles</i>, so <i>cross</i> is correct; the phrase beginning <i>as well as</i> is an interruption that needs a comma on both sides.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-04", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-04", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>Among the artifacts recovered from the sunken merchant ship was a bronze compass, several glass bottles, and a chest of silver coins.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>artifacts</i> to <i>artifact</i>.", "Change <i>recovered</i> to <i>recovers</i>.", "Change <i>was</i> to <i>were</i>.", "Delete the comma after <i>compass</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects the sentence. The sentence is inverted: its subject comes after the verb and is the compound <i>a bronze compass, several glass bottles, and a chest of silver coins</i>, which requires the plural verb <i>were</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> <i>Among</i> requires a plural object, so <i>artifacts</i> is correct; it is also not the subject of the sentence.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> <i>Recovered</i> is a participle describing the artifacts, not the main verb; <i>recovers</i> would make no sense.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> Turn the sentence around to find the subject: <i>A bronze compass, several glass bottles, and a chest of silver coins were among the artifacts</i>. Three items joined by <i>and</i> are plural, even though the first item, <i>a bronze compass</i>, is singular and sits next to the verb.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The comma after <i>compass</i> separates items in a series and must stay.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-05", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-05", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) A colony of honeybees, which may contain as many as 60,000 insects, depend on a single queen to lay all of its eggs. (2) The workers, each of which lives only a few weeks in summer, gather nectar and build the wax comb. (3) There are also a few hundred drones, male bees whose only task is to mate with a queen. (4) The number of bees in a hive rises through the spring and reaches its peak in early summer.</blockquote><p>Which sentence contains an error in subject-verb agreement?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence has a verb that does not agree with its subject. In sentence 1 the subject is the singular noun <i>colony</i>, so <i>depend</i> should be <i>depends</i>.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> The subject <i>colony</i> is singular, as the pronoun <i>its</i> later in the sentence confirms. The plural nouns <i>honeybees</i> and <i>insects</i> come between the subject and the verb, but the sentence should read <i>A colony … depends on a single queen</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2 the plural subject <i>workers</i> correctly takes <i>gather</i> and <i>build</i>; inside the interrupting clause, the singular <i>each</i> correctly takes <i>lives</i>.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 3 the subject follows the verb: <i>a few hundred drones</i> is plural, so <i>There are</i> is correct, and the singular <i>task</i> correctly takes <i>is</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 4 <i>The number</i> is singular, so <i>rises</i> and <i>reaches</i> are correct even though the plural <i>bees</i> stands beside them.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-06", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-06", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Every spring, the students at Lincoln Middle School hold a science fair in the gymnasium. (2) Each of the projects is judged by a panel of teachers, and the winners receive ribbons. (3) This year, neither the judges nor the principal were prepared for the size of the crowd, and there was not enough chairs for half of the visitors. (4) A team of eighth graders, along with their science teacher, has offered to plan next year’s event.</blockquote><p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Change <i>students</i> to <i>student’s</i> AND change <i>hold</i> to <i>holds</i>.", "Sentence 2: Change <i>is</i> to <i>are</i> AND delete the comma after <i>teachers</i>.", "Sentence 3: Change <i>were</i> to <i>was</i> AND change <i>there was</i> to <i>there were</i>.", "Sentence 4: Change <i>has</i> to <i>have</i> AND delete the comma after <i>teacher</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence contains two errors that the paired revisions would fix. Sentence 3 has two verbs that disagree with their subjects: <i>were</i> should be <i>was</i> to agree with <i>principal</i>, and <i>there was</i> should be <i>there were</i> to agree with <i>chairs</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> <i>Students</i> is a plural subject, not a possessive, and it correctly takes the plural verb <i>hold</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The subject <i>Each</i> is singular, so <i>is judged</i> is correct, and the comma before <i>and</i> correctly separates two complete sentences.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> With <i>neither … nor</i> the verb agrees with the nearer subject, the singular <i>principal</i>: <i>neither the judges nor the principal was prepared</i>. After <i>there</i>, the verb agrees with the noun that follows it, the plural <i>chairs</i>: <i>there were not enough chairs</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The subject is the singular <i>team</i>; the phrase <i>along with their science teacher</i> does not make it plural, so <i>has offered</i> is correct, and the interrupting phrase needs a comma on both sides.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-07", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-07", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>Each of the murals that decorate the walls of the subway station depict a scene from the neighborhood’s history.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>murals</i> to <i>mural</i>.", "Change <i>decorate</i> to <i>decorates</i>.", "Change <i>walls</i> to <i>wall</i>.", "Change <i>depict</i> to <i>depicts</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects the sentence. The subject of the main verb is the singular pronoun <i>Each</i>, so the verb must be <i>depicts</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> <i>Each of the</i> must be followed by a plural noun, so <i>murals</i> is correct.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> <i>That</i> refers to the plural <i>murals</i>, so <i>decorate</i> is already correct.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Nothing is wrong with <i>walls</i>, and changing it would not repair the verb.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> Remove the words between the subject and the verb and the error is plain: <i>Each … depicts a scene</i>. The plural verb <i>decorate</i> earlier in the sentence belongs to <i>murals</i>, not to <i>Each</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-08", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-08", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) The New York City Marathon, which winds through all five boroughs, attracts more than 50,000 runners each November. (2) Lining the streets along the 26.2-mile course is more than a million spectators, many of whom arrive hours before the first runners pass. (3) The crew of volunteers at each aid station hands out water and sports drinks to the runners. (4) Neither the steep bridges nor the unpredictable autumn weather discourages the thousands of people who apply for a place in the race each year.</blockquote><p>Which sentence contains an error in subject-verb agreement?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence has a verb that does not agree with its subject. Sentence 2 is inverted: its subject, <i>more than a million spectators</i>, follows the verb and is plural, so <i>is</i> should be <i>are</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1 the singular subject <i>Marathon</i> correctly takes <i>attracts</i>, and <i>which</i>, referring to the Marathon, correctly takes <i>winds</i>.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Turn the sentence around to find the subject: <i>More than a million spectators are lining the streets</i>. The singular noun <i>course</i> just before the verb is part of the phrase <i>along the 26.2-mile course</i>, not the subject.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 3 the subject is the singular <i>crew</i>, so <i>hands</i> is correct even though <i>volunteers</i> is plural.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 4 the verb agrees with the nearer subject after <i>nor</i>, the singular <i>weather</i>, so <i>discourages</i> is correct; <i>who</i> refers to the plural <i>people</i>, so <i>apply</i> is correct.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-09", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-09", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) The Metropolitan Museum of Art, one of the largest museums in the world, houses more than a million works. (2) Its collection of Egyptian artifacts includes the Temple of Dendur, which was shipped to New York in pieces. (3) There are also galleries devoted to armor, musical instruments, and photography. (4) Each of the museum’s seventeen departments employ curators; who decide which objects are displayed.</blockquote><p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Delete the comma after <i>Art</i> AND change <i>houses</i> to <i>house</i>.", "Sentence 2: Change <i>includes</i> to <i>include</i> AND change <i>was</i> to <i>were</i>.", "Sentence 3: Change <i>are</i> to <i>is</i> AND delete the comma after <i>armor</i>.", "Sentence 4: Change <i>employ</i> to <i>employs</i> AND change the semicolon to a comma."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence contains two errors that the paired revisions would fix. In sentence 4 the singular subject <i>Each</i> needs the verb <i>employs</i>, and the semicolon is wrong because <i>who decide which objects are displayed</i> is not a complete sentence.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The commas around <i>one of the largest museums in the world</i> correctly set off an interrupting phrase, and the singular subject <i>Museum</i> correctly takes <i>houses</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The subject <i>collection</i> is singular, so <i>includes</i> is correct, and <i>which</i> refers to the singular <i>Temple of Dendur</i>, so <i>was shipped</i> is correct.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The subject follows the verb: <i>galleries</i> is plural, so <i>There are</i> is correct, and the comma after <i>armor</i> separates items in a series.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> <i>Each</i> is singular no matter how many <i>departments</i> follow it: <i>Each … employs curators</i>. A semicolon must have a complete sentence on both sides, and the clause beginning with <i>who</i> cannot stand alone, so a comma is needed. <i>Decide</i> is correct because <i>who</i> refers to the plural <i>curators</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-10", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-10", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>The lead violinist, as well as the three cellists who sit beside her, have been invited to perform at the citywide festival next month.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>have</i> to <i>has</i>.", "Change <i>sit</i> to <i>sits</i>.", "Change <i>cellists</i> to <i>cellist</i>.", "Delete the comma after <i>her</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects the sentence. The subject is the singular <i>violinist</i>; a phrase beginning with <i>as well as</i> does not make the subject plural, so the verb must be <i>has</i>.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> Unlike <i>and</i>, the words <i>as well as</i> do not create a compound subject. The phrase between the commas can be lifted out, leaving <i>The lead violinist … has been invited</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> <i>Who</i> refers to the plural <i>cellists</i>, so <i>sit</i> is already correct.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> <i>Three</i> requires the plural <i>cellists</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The interrupting phrase needs a comma at each end; deleting the second one would leave it unclosed.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-11", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-11", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Completed in 1825, the Erie Canal connects the Hudson River to Lake Erie. (2) The system of locks along the route raises and lowers boats over a climb of more than 500 feet between Albany and Buffalo. (3) The cost of shipping grain and other goods from the Midwest to New York City were reduced by nearly ninety percent once the canal opened. (4) There were few engineers in the country with any experience in building canals, so many of the workers learned their trade on the job.</blockquote><p>Which sentence contains an error in subject-verb agreement?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence has a verb that does not agree with its subject. In sentence 3 the subject is the singular noun <i>cost</i>, so <i>were reduced</i> should be <i>was reduced</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1 the singular subject <i>Erie Canal</i> correctly takes <i>connects</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2 the subject is the singular <i>system</i>, so <i>raises and lowers</i> is correct even though <i>locks</i> is plural.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> Everything between <i>cost</i> and the verb, including the plural <i>goods</i>, belongs to the phrase <i>of shipping grain and other goods from the Midwest to New York City</i>. The sentence should read <i>The cost … was reduced</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 4 the subject follows the verb: <i>few engineers</i> is plural, so <i>There were</i> is correct, and the plural <i>many of the workers</i> correctly takes <i>learned</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:agreement-12", "form": "Extra", "n": "agreement-12", "tag": "agreement", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>Either the coach or the two team captains decides which players will start, and the list of starters is posted outside the gym every Friday.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>captains</i> to <i>captain</i>.", "Change <i>decides</i> to <i>decide</i>.", "Change <i>is</i> to <i>are</i>.", "Change <i>start</i> to <i>starts</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects the sentence. When subjects are joined by <i>either … or</i>, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, here the plural <i>captains</i>, so the verb must be <i>decide</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> <i>Two</i> requires the plural <i>captains</i>.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> The subject closer to the verb is <i>the two team captains</i>, so the plural <i>decide</i> is needed: <i>Either the coach or the two team captains decide</i>. The singular <i>coach</i> is farther from the verb and does not control it.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The subject of the second clause is the singular <i>list</i>, not <i>starters</i>, so <i>is posted</i> is correct.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> After the helping verb <i>will</i>, the base form <i>start</i> is required.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:modifier-01", "form": "Extra", "n": "modifier-01", "tag": "modifier", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>Buried beneath volcanic ash for nearly 1,700 years, archaeologists found the buildings of Pompeii remarkably well preserved.</blockquote><p>How should this sentence be revised?</p>", "opts": ["Having been buried beneath volcanic ash for nearly 1,700 years, archaeologists found that the buildings of Pompeii were remarkably well preserved.", "Buried beneath volcanic ash for nearly 1,700 years, the buildings of Pompeii were remarkably well preserved when archaeologists found them.", "Archaeologists, buried beneath volcanic ash for nearly 1,700 years, found the buildings of Pompeii remarkably well preserved.", "Remarkably well preserved, archaeologists found the buildings of Pompeii buried beneath volcanic ash for nearly 1,700 years."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the correct revision of the sentence in the box. The sentence is incorrect because the opening phrase <i>Buried beneath volcanic ash for nearly 1,700 years</i> is followed by <i>archaeologists</i>, so the sentence says that the archaeologists were buried. An opening phrase of this kind describes the subject that comes right after the comma.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Changing <i>Buried</i> to <i>Having been buried</i> does not change what the phrase describes. The subject after the comma is still <i>archaeologists</i>.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> The subject after the comma is now <i>the buildings of Pompeii</i>, which is what was buried, and the rest of the sentence keeps the original meaning.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Placing the phrase directly after <i>Archaeologists</i> states even more plainly that the archaeologists were buried.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The new opening phrase, <i>Remarkably well preserved</i>, now describes <i>archaeologists</i>, which creates a second modifier error in place of the first.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:modifier-02", "form": "Extra", "n": "modifier-02", "tag": "modifier", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>To measure the height of a distant mountain, a device called a theodolite was relied on by nineteenth-century surveyors.</blockquote><p>How should this sentence be revised?</p>", "opts": ["To measure the height of a distant mountain, a device called a theodolite was what nineteenth-century surveyors relied on.", "A device called a theodolite, to measure the height of a distant mountain, was relied on by nineteenth-century surveyors.", "To measure the height of a distant mountain, the reliance of nineteenth-century surveyors was on a device called a theodolite.", "To measure the height of a distant mountain, nineteenth-century surveyors relied on a device called a theodolite."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the correct revision of the sentence in the box. The sentence is incorrect because the opening phrase <i>To measure the height of a distant mountain</i> names a purpose that belongs to the surveyors, but the subject that follows the comma is <i>a device called a theodolite</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The subject after the comma is still <i>a device called a theodolite</i>, so the phrase still does not attach to the people doing the measuring, and <i>was what ... relied on</i> is wordy.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Moving the phrase to the middle interrupts the main clause awkwardly and still leaves the sentence in the passive voice with the surveyors at the end, far from the phrase that describes their purpose.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The subject after the comma is now <i>the reliance</i>, and a reliance cannot measure a mountain.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> The subject after the comma is <i>nineteenth-century surveyors</i>, the people who measure, and the active verb <i>relied on</i> makes the sentence direct.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:modifier-03", "form": "Extra", "n": "modifier-03", "tag": "modifier", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>A hiker spotted a bald eagle with binoculars that was perched on a dead pine near the reservoir.</blockquote><p>How should this sentence be revised?</p>", "opts": ["Using binoculars, a hiker spotted a bald eagle that was perched on a dead pine near the reservoir.", "A hiker spotted a bald eagle that was perched on a dead pine near the reservoir using binoculars.", "Perched on a dead pine near the reservoir, a hiker using binoculars spotted a bald eagle.", "A hiker spotted a bald eagle near the reservoir with binoculars that was perched on a dead pine."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the correct revision of the sentence in the box. The sentence is incorrect because <i>with binoculars</i> sits next to <i>a bald eagle</i>, as if the eagle had the binoculars, and because the clause <i>that was perched on a dead pine</i> follows <i>binoculars</i>, as if the binoculars were perched in the tree.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> <i>Using binoculars</i> comes directly before <i>a hiker</i>, the person using them, and <i>that was perched on a dead pine</i> comes directly after <i>a bald eagle</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The clause now follows <i>eagle</i>, but <i>using binoculars</i> is stranded at the end next to <i>the reservoir</i>, far from the hiker it describes.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The opening phrase <i>Perched on a dead pine near the reservoir</i> describes the subject after the comma, <i>a hiker</i>, so the sentence says that the hiker was perched in the tree.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The clause <i>that was perched on a dead pine</i> still follows <i>binoculars</i>, and the sentence now says that the reservoir has the binoculars.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:modifier-04", "form": "Extra", "n": "modifier-04", "tag": "modifier", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>While restoring the old subway station, a mosaic hidden since 1945 was uncovered behind a tiled wall by workers.</blockquote><p>How should this sentence be revised?</p>", "opts": ["While restoring the old subway station, workers’ efforts uncovered a mosaic that had been hidden behind a tiled wall since 1945.", "While restoring the old subway station, behind a tiled wall a mosaic hidden since 1945 was uncovered by workers.", "While restoring the old subway station, workers uncovered a mosaic that had been hidden behind a tiled wall since 1945.", "Workers uncovered a mosaic while restoring the old subway station that had been hidden behind a tiled wall since 1945."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the correct revision of the sentence in the box. The sentence is incorrect because the opening phrase <i>While restoring the old subway station</i> is followed by <i>a mosaic</i>, so the sentence says that the mosaic was restoring the station.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The subject after the comma is <i>efforts</i>, not <i>workers</i>. The possessive <i>workers’</i> only describes the efforts, so the sentence says that the efforts were restoring the station.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Inserting <i>behind a tiled wall</i> after the comma does not change the subject, which is still <i>a mosaic</i>.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> The subject after the comma is <i>workers</i>, the people doing the restoring, and <i>that had been hidden behind a tiled wall since 1945</i> directly follows <i>a mosaic</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The clause <i>that had been hidden behind a tiled wall since 1945</i> now follows <i>the old subway station</i>, so the sentence says that the station, not the mosaic, had been hidden.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:modifier-05", "form": "Extra", "n": "modifier-05", "tag": "modifier", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Completed in 1825, the Erie Canal connected the Hudson River at Albany with Lake Erie at Buffalo. (2) Before the canal opened, moving a ton of grain overland from Buffalo to New York City had taken roughly three weeks and had cost about one hundred dollars. (3) Traveling by canal boat instead, the cost of the same shipment fell to less than ten dollars. (4) To pay for the project, New York State collected tolls from every boat that used the waterway.</blockquote><p>Which sentence contains an error in its construction and should be revised?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the identification of the sentence that contains an error in its construction. An opening descriptive phrase must describe the subject that follows the comma.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1, <i>Completed in 1825</i> correctly describes the subject, <i>the Erie Canal</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 2 opens with a full clause, <i>Before the canal opened</i>, which has its own subject. The past perfect verbs <i>had taken</i> and <i>had cost</i> are correct for a time before the canal opened.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> In sentence 3, <i>Traveling by canal boat instead</i> is followed by the subject <i>the cost</i>, but a cost does not travel by boat; the shipment does. A revised version might read: <i>When the same shipment traveled by canal boat instead, its cost fell to less than ten dollars.</i></p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 4, <i>To pay for the project</i> correctly describes the purpose of the subject, <i>New York State</i>, which collected the tolls.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:modifier-06", "form": "Extra", "n": "modifier-06", "tag": "modifier", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Weighing less than a paper clip, scientists are still amazed that a monarch butterfly can fly as far as 3,000 miles to reach its winter home in central Mexico. (2) Guided by the position of the sun, the butterflies hold a steady southwesterly course for weeks. (3) To conserve energy, they glide on rising columns of warm air whenever conditions allow. (4) The monarchs that complete the journey have never seen the mountain forests where they will spend the winter, for they hatched long after the previous year’s migrants had left Mexico.</blockquote><p>Which sentence contains an error in its construction and should be revised?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the identification of the sentence that contains an error in its construction. An opening descriptive phrase must describe the subject that follows the comma.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> In sentence 1, <i>Weighing less than a paper clip</i> is followed by the subject <i>scientists</i>, so the sentence says that the scientists weigh less than a paper clip. The butterfly is mentioned only later, inside the clause beginning with <i>that</i>. A revised version might read: <i>Scientists are still amazed that a monarch butterfly, weighing less than a paper clip, can fly as far as 3,000 miles ...</i></p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2, <i>Guided by the position of the sun</i> correctly describes the subject, <i>the butterflies</i>.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 3, <i>To conserve energy</i> correctly gives the purpose of the subject, <i>they</i>, which refers to the butterflies.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 4 is long, but each clause follows the noun it describes: <i>that complete the journey</i> follows <i>monarchs</i>, and <i>where they will spend the winter</i> follows <i>forests</i>. The comma before <i>for</i> correctly joins two complete clauses.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:modifier-07", "form": "Extra", "n": "modifier-07", "tag": "modifier", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Opened in 1883, the Brooklyn Bridge was the first fixed crossing over the East River. (2) Injured in an accident while surveying the site, John Roebling, the bridge’s designer, died before construction began. (3) His son Washington took charge of the project, but an illness caused by working in the pressurized chambers beneath the river soon confined him to his apartment. (4) Watching the work through a telescope from his window, Washington’s instructions were carried to the engineers each day by his wife, Emily.</blockquote><p>Which sentence contains an error in its construction and should be revised?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the identification of the sentence that contains an error in its construction. An opening descriptive phrase must describe the subject that follows the comma.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1, <i>Opened in 1883</i> correctly describes the subject, <i>the Brooklyn Bridge</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2, <i>Injured in an accident while surveying the site</i> correctly describes the subject, <i>John Roebling</i>. The phrase <i>the bridge’s designer</i> is correctly set off by commas as extra information about him.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 3, <i>caused by working in the pressurized chambers beneath the river</i> directly follows <i>an illness</i>, the noun it describes.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> In sentence 4, <i>Watching the work through a telescope from his window</i> is followed by the subject <i>instructions</i>. The possessive <i>Washington’s</i> only describes the instructions, so the sentence says that the instructions were watching through a telescope. A revised version might read: <i>Watching the work through a telescope from his window, Washington gave instructions that his wife, Emily, carried to the engineers each day.</i></p>"}, {"id": "gg:modifier-08", "form": "Extra", "n": "modifier-08", "tag": "modifier", "pid": null, "html": "<p>A sportswriter wants to state that, in its first season, a new team was defeated in all but a few of the games it played. Which sentence expresses this meaning correctly?</p>", "opts": ["The team nearly lost every game it played in its first season.", "The team lost nearly every game it played in its first season.", "The team lost every game it nearly played in its first season.", "The team lost every game it played in nearly its first season."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks which placement of the limiting word <i>nearly</i> gives the intended meaning, that the team lost all but a few of its games. A word such as <i>nearly</i>, <i>almost</i>, or <i>only</i> limits the word or phrase that comes right after it.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Here <i>nearly</i> comes before <i>lost</i>, so the sentence says that the team came close to losing every game, which could mean that it did not actually lose any of them.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Here <i>nearly</i> comes before <i>every game</i>, so the sentence says that the number of games the team lost was close to all of them.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Here <i>nearly</i> comes before <i>played</i>, so the sentence says that the team lost games that it came close to playing but did not play.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Here <i>nearly</i> comes before <i>its first season</i>, so the sentence refers to a period that was not quite the first season and says that the team lost every game in it.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-01", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-01", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) In 1883 Emily Roebling became the first person to cross the Brooklyn Bridge, a structure that she had helped to complete. (2) Her husband, Washington Roebling, had taken over as chief engineer from his father, John, after he was injured in an accident at the waterfront. (3) When illness later confined Washington to his bedroom, Emily carried his instructions to the workers and studied engineering so that she could answer their questions. (4) Today a plaque on one of the bridge’s towers honors Emily and the years that she devoted to the project.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct a vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence in which a pronoun could refer to more than one person. In sentence 2, the pronoun <i>he</i> in <i>after he was injured</i> follows the names of two men, Washington Roebling and his father, John, so the reader cannot tell which of them was injured.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1, <i>she</i> can refer only to Emily Roebling, the only person named in the sentence.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 2 names two men before the pronoun <i>he</i>, and either one could sensibly have been injured. Replacing <i>he</i> with <i>John</i> (or <i>the older man</i>) would make the sentence clear.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 3 has several pronouns, but each has one possible antecedent: <i>his</i> refers to Washington, the only man in the sentence; <i>she</i> refers to Emily; and <i>their</i> refers to <i>the workers</i>, the only plural noun that can ask questions.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 4, <i>she</i> can refer only to Emily.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-02", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-02", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) A honeybee that finds a rich patch of flowers returns to its hive and performs what scientists call a waggle dance. (2) As the bee moves in a figure eight, it shakes its body to show the other workers how far away the food is. (3) The workers crowd around the dancer, and they follow its movements with their antennae. (4) Researchers once placed a dish of sugar water near a hive, and within minutes it was covered with bees.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct a vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence in which a pronoun does not clearly point to one noun. In sentence 4, <i>it</i> could refer to <i>a dish of sugar water</i> or to <i>a hive</i>, since either one could be covered with bees.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1, <i>its</i> can refer only to <i>a honeybee</i>, the one singular noun that could have a hive.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2, <i>it</i> and <i>its</i> both refer to <i>the bee</i>; a figure eight cannot shake a body.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 3 looks crowded with pronouns, but number keeps them apart: the plural <i>they</i> and <i>their</i> must refer to <i>the workers</i>, and the singular <i>its</i> must refer to <i>the dancer</i>.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 4 places two singular nouns, <i>dish</i> and <i>hive</i>, before <i>it</i>, and the sentence makes sense with either. Replacing <i>it</i> with <i>the dish</i> would correct the problem.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-03", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-03", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) In the article about the new recycling plant in Brooklyn, they say that more than 800 tons of material arrive every day. (2) Trucks dump their loads onto a moving belt, which carries the material past a series of machines. (3) A powerful magnet lifts steel cans off the belt, and a blast of air pushes them into a separate bin. (4) Workers then inspect the remaining paper and remove any plastic bags that they find in it.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct a vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence in which a pronoun has no clear antecedent. In sentence 1, <i>they</i> does not refer to any noun at all: neither <i>the article</i> nor <i>the new recycling plant</i> is plural, and no people have been mentioned.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 1 uses <i>they</i> for some unnamed people. The sentence should name them, as in <i>The article about the new recycling plant in Brooklyn says that</i> or <i>the reporters say that</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2, <i>their</i> can refer only to <i>trucks</i>, and <i>which</i> directly follows <i>a moving belt</i>, the noun it describes.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 3, <i>them</i> is plural, and <i>steel cans</i> is the only plural noun in the sentence.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 4 has two pronouns, but each is clear: <i>they</i> refers to <i>workers</i>, since plastic bags cannot find anything, and the singular <i>it</i> refers to <i>the remaining paper</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-04", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-04", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Matthew Henson was working in a Washington hat shop in 1887 when he met the explorer Robert Peary. (2) Peary soon hired the young man for a surveying trip to Nicaragua, and the two later made several voyages to the Arctic, where they spent years searching for a route to the North Pole. (3) During these voyages Henson told Peary that he should learn to drive a dogsled as the Inuit hunters did. (4) In 1909 the members of the final expedition reached what they believed to be the pole.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct a vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence in which a pronoun could refer to more than one person. In sentence 3, <i>he</i> could refer to Henson or to Peary: Henson may have been speaking about his own need to learn or giving advice to Peary.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1, <i>he</i> comes after only one name, Matthew Henson; Peary has not yet been mentioned, and he is the person being met.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2, <i>they</i> refers to <i>the two</i>, the men who made the voyages together. No other plural noun could search for a route.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 3 names both men before <i>he</i>, and nothing in the sentence shows which one should learn to drive a dogsled. Replacing <i>he</i> with a name would correct the problem.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 4, <i>they</i> can refer only to <i>the members of the final expedition</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-05", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-05", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) When New York’s first subway line opened in 1904, it ran about nine miles from City Hall to 145th Street. (2) Mayor George McClellan stood beside the motorman on the first train, and he drove it most of the way to Harlem. (3) More than 100,000 people paid a nickel that evening so that they could ride the new trains. (4) Although the fare has risen many times since then, the subway still carries millions of riders to their jobs each day.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct a vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence in which a pronoun could refer to more than one noun. In sentence 2, <i>he</i> could refer to <i>Mayor George McClellan</i> or to <i>the motorman</i>, so the reader cannot tell who drove the train.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1, <i>it</i> refers to <i>New York’s first subway line</i>, the only thing in the sentence that could run from one place to another.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 2 mentions two men before <i>he</i>. Driving a train is a motorman’s job, but the mayor is the subject of the sentence, so either reading is reasonable. (The pronoun <i>it</i> is clear, since it can refer only to <i>the first train</i>.) Replacing <i>he</i> with <i>the mayor</i> would correct the problem.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 3, <i>they</i> can refer only to <i>people</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 4, <i>their</i> refers to <i>riders</i>; the singular nouns <i>fare</i> and <i>subway</i> cannot be the antecedent of a plural pronoun.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-06", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-06", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) The sculptor Augusta Savage opened a studio in Harlem in the 1930s, and she offered free art classes there to anyone who wanted them. (2) One of her students, Jacob Lawrence, later became famous for the paintings that he made of the Great Migration. (3) Savage created a sixteen-foot sculpture for the 1939 World’s Fair, where it was seen by millions of visitors. (4) After the fair closed, workers removed the sculpture from the pavilion and demolished it.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct a vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence in which a pronoun could refer to more than one noun. In sentence 4, <i>it</i> could refer to <i>the sculpture</i> or to <i>the pavilion</i>, since workers could have demolished either one.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 1, <i>she</i> refers to Augusta Savage, and the plural <i>them</i> refers to <i>free art classes</i>, the only plural noun that someone could want.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 2, <i>her</i> refers to Savage, the only woman mentioned in the paragraph, and <i>he</i> refers to Jacob Lawrence.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> In sentence 3, <i>it</i> refers to the <i>sculpture</i>; the fair is the place where the object was seen, so it cannot be the thing seen there.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 4 places two singular nouns, <i>sculpture</i> and <i>pavilion</i>, before <i>it</i>, and both are things that can be demolished. Replacing <i>it</i> with <i>the artwork</i> would correct the problem.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-07", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-07", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>Each of the bridges that cross the East River was praised as an engineering marvel when their roadway first opened to traffic.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>cross</i> to <i>crosses</i>.", "Change <i>was</i> to <i>were</i>.", "Change <i>their</i> to <i>its</i>.", "Change <i>opened</i> to <i>opens</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects an error in pronoun-antecedent agreement. The subject of the sentence is the singular pronoun <i>Each</i>, so the pronoun that refers back to it must be the singular <i>its</i>, not the plural <i>their</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The verb <i>cross</i> belongs to the clause <i>that cross the East River</i>, in which <i>that</i> stands for the plural noun <i>bridges</i>. The plural verb is correct.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The verb <i>was</i> agrees with the singular subject <i>Each</i>, not with the nearby plural noun <i>bridges</i>. Changing it would create an agreement error.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> <i>Each</i> is singular, as the verb <i>was</i> confirms, so the sentence should say that each bridge was praised when <i>its</i> roadway opened.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The sentence describes past events, so the past-tense verb <i>opened</i> is correct.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-08", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-08", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>The committee that reviews applications for the city’s summer internships has posted its schedule, and they will announce the winners in May.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>they</i> to <i>it</i>.", "Change <i>its</i> to <i>their</i>.", "Change <i>reviews</i> to <i>review</i>.", "Change <i>has</i> to <i>have</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects an error in pronoun-antecedent agreement. The collective noun <i>committee</i> is treated as a single unit throughout the sentence (<i>reviews</i>, <i>has posted</i>, <i>its schedule</i>), so the plural pronoun <i>they</i> must be changed to <i>it</i>.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> <i>They</i> refers to <i>the committee</i>, which the sentence treats as singular. Changing <i>they</i> to <i>it</i> makes every verb and pronoun agree.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> <i>Its</i> correctly matches the singular verbs <i>reviews</i> and <i>has</i>. Changing it to <i>their</i> would add a second mismatched pronoun instead of fixing the first.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The verb <i>reviews</i> agrees with <i>committee</i>, a singular noun. The plural noun <i>applications</i> is the object of the verb, not its subject.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The verb <i>has</i> agrees with the singular subject <i>committee</i>, not with the plural noun <i>internships</i> that comes just before it.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-09", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-09", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>The red-tailed hawk, like many other birds of prey that hunt in the city’s parks, often returns to the same nest that they built the year before.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>returns</i> to <i>return</i>.", "Change <i>hunt</i> to <i>hunts</i>.", "Change <i>built</i> to <i>build</i>.", "Change <i>they</i> to <i>it</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects an error in pronoun-antecedent agreement. The pronoun <i>they</i> refers to the subject, <i>the red-tailed hawk</i>, which is singular, so the pronoun must be the singular <i>it</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The subject of <i>returns</i> is the singular <i>hawk</i>. The phrase <i>like many other birds of prey</i> is set off by commas and does not make the subject plural.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The verb <i>hunt</i> belongs to the clause <i>that hunt in the city’s parks</i>, in which <i>that</i> stands for the plural <i>birds of prey</i>. The plural verb is correct.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The nest was made <i>the year before</i>, so the past-tense verb <i>built</i> is correct.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> The hawk that returns is the one that made the nest. Because <i>hawk</i> is singular (<i>returns</i>), the sentence should read <i>the same nest that it built the year before</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-10", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-10", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>When the curator showed the photographer the damaged print, she offered to restore it at no charge.</blockquote><p>Which revision corrects the vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["When she showed the photographer the damaged print, she offered to restore it at no charge.", "When the curator showed the photographer the damaged print, the photographer offered to restore it at no charge.", "When the curator showed her the damaged print, she offered to restore it at no charge.", "The curator showed the photographer the damaged print, the photographer offered to restore it at no charge."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the revision that makes clear who offered to restore the print. In the original sentence, <i>she</i> could refer to <i>the curator</i> or to <i>the photographer</i>. Option B replaces <i>she</i> with <i>the photographer</i> and leaves the rest of the sentence correct.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> This revision replaces <i>the curator</i> with a second <i>she</i>, so now neither pronoun has a clear antecedent.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Naming <i>the photographer</i> removes the doubt. The remaining pronoun, <i>it</i>, can refer only to <i>the damaged print</i>.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This revision replaces <i>the photographer</i> with <i>her</i>, which has no antecedent, and <i>she</i> is still unclear.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> This revision names the photographer, but it joins two complete sentences with only a comma, which creates a run-on.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-11", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-11", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>The technicians lifted the mirror out of the telescope so that it could be cleaned.</blockquote><p>Which revision corrects the vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["The technicians lifted the mirror out of the telescope so that this could be cleaned.", "So that it could be cleaned, the technicians lifted the mirror out of the telescope.", "The technicians lifted the mirror out of the telescope so that the mirror could be cleaned.", "The technicians lifted the mirror out of the telescope so that they could be cleaned."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the revision that makes clear what was to be cleaned. In the original sentence, <i>it</i> could refer to <i>the mirror</i> or to <i>the telescope</i>. Option C replaces <i>it</i> with <i>the mirror</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> <i>This</i> is no clearer than <i>it</i>; the word could still point to the mirror or to the telescope.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Moving the clause to the front of the sentence does not help. <i>It</i> still has two possible antecedents, and now it comes before both of them.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> Repeating the noun <i>the mirror</i> tells the reader exactly what was cleaned, and the revision adds no new error.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The only plural noun that <i>they</i> can refer to is <i>the technicians</i>, so this revision changes the meaning of the sentence.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:pronoun-12", "form": "Extra", "n": "pronoun-12", "tag": "pronoun", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>Although the governor and the senator disagreed about the new bridge, he promised to support the project.</blockquote><p>Which revision corrects the vague pronoun?</p>", "opts": ["Although the governor and the senator disagreed about the new bridge, the senator promised to support the project.", "Although he and the senator disagreed about the new bridge, he promised to support the project.", "The governor and the senator disagreed about the new bridge, however, the senator promised to support the project.", "Although the governor and the senator disagreed about the new bridge, that one promised to support the project."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the revision that makes clear who made the promise. In the original sentence, <i>he</i> could refer to <i>the governor</i> or to <i>the senator</i>. Option A replaces <i>he</i> with <i>the senator</i> and changes nothing else.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> Naming <i>the senator</i> removes the doubt, and the sentence is still correctly built: a dependent clause beginning with <i>Although</i>, a comma, and an independent clause.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> This revision removes <i>the governor</i>, so the first <i>he</i> has no antecedent, and the second <i>he</i> is as unclear as before.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> This revision names the senator, but it creates a run-on. When <i>however</i> joins two complete sentences, it needs a semicolon or a period before it, not a comma.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> <i>That one</i> does not tell the reader which of the two men is meant.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-01", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-01", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p><blockquote>(1) When the first subway line in New York City opened in 1904, more than 100,000 people paid a nickel to ride it on the first evening alone. (2) The line ran about nine miles from City Hall to 145th Street; it cut a trip that had taken more than an hour by streetcar to less than half an hour. (3) Each of the crews that dug the tunnels were confronted by the same three obstacles, solid bedrock, tangled water mains, and the foundations of nearby buildings. (4) The system has been rebuilt many times since then; however, several of the original stations are still in use.</blockquote>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Delete the comma after <i>1904</i> AND change <i>paid</i> to <i>pay</i>.", "Sentence 2: Change the semicolon after <i>Street</i> to a comma AND change <i>had taken</i> to <i>takes</i>.", "Sentence 3: Change <i>were</i> to <i>was</i> AND change the comma after <i>obstacles</i> to a colon.", "Sentence 4: Change the semicolon after <i>then</i> to a comma AND delete the comma after <i>however</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the pair of revisions needed to correct the errors in the paragraph, both of which appear in sentence 3.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “1904” is needed to separate the introductory clause “When the first subway line in New York City opened in 1904” from the main clause. The verb “paid” is correctly in the past tense, which the paragraph uses for the events of 1904.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The semicolon after “Street” correctly joins two complete sentences; a comma there would create a run-on. The past perfect “had taken” is correct because it describes how long the trip took before the line opened, an earlier time than the past-tense “cut.”</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> The subject of the sentence is the singular pronoun “Each,” not the plural noun “crews” in the phrase that follows it, so the verb must be the singular “was confronted.” In addition, the words before the list (“Each of the crews that dug the tunnels was confronted by the same three obstacles”) form a complete sentence that introduces a list, so a colon is needed after “obstacles.” With a comma, the three obstacles run together with the items of the list.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The semicolon after “then” is required because “however” is not a conjunction and cannot join two complete sentences with only a comma. The comma after “however” is also required to set off the transition from the sentence that follows it.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-02", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-02", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p><blockquote>(1) Each of the bees in a honeybee colony belong to one of three groups; the queen, the workers, or the drones. (2) The queen, which can live for several years, lays as many as 2,000 eggs a day during the spring. (3) The workers do nearly everything else: they build the comb, feed the larvae, guard the entrance, and gather nectar. (4) Drones do not gather food or defend the hive; their only task is to mate with a queen from another colony.</blockquote>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Change <i>belong</i> to <i>belongs</i> AND change the semicolon after <i>groups</i> to a colon.", "Sentence 2: Delete the comma after <i>years</i> AND change <i>lays</i> to <i>lay</i>.", "Sentence 3: Change the colon after <i>else</i> to a comma AND change <i>gather</i> to <i>gathers</i>.", "Sentence 4: Change the semicolon after <i>hive</i> to a comma AND change <i>their</i> to <i>its</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the pair of revisions needed to correct the errors in the paragraph, both of which appear in sentence 1.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> The subject of the sentence is the singular pronoun “Each,” not the plural noun “bees,” so the verb must be the singular “belongs.” A semicolon must be followed by a complete sentence, but “the queen, the workers, or the drones” is only a list. Because the words before the list form a complete sentence that introduces it, a colon is needed after “groups.”</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “years” is needed to close the nonrestrictive clause “which can live for several years,” which is already set off by a comma after “queen.” The singular verb “lays” agrees with the singular subject “queen.”</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The colon after “else” correctly follows a complete sentence and introduces an explanation of it; a comma there would create a run-on. The verb “gather” is correct because its subject is the plural pronoun “they,” which also governs “build,” “feed,” and “guard.”</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The semicolon after “hive” correctly joins two closely related complete sentences; a comma there would create a run-on. The plural “their” correctly refers to the plural noun “Drones.”</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-03", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-03", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p><blockquote>(1) Before the Erie Canal opened in 1825, hauling a ton of grain from Buffalo to New York City took about two weeks and cost roughly one hundred dollars. (2) The canal changed those figures dramatically: the same shipment could now arrive in about five days for roughly ten dollars. (3) Towns along the route, such as Syracuse and Rochester, grew quickly; New York City, meanwhile, became the busiest port in the nation. (4) The canal’s success inspired other states to dig waterways of its own, however, the spread of railroads in the 1840s soon made most of them unprofitable.</blockquote>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Delete the comma after <i>1825</i> AND change <i>took</i> to <i>takes</i>.", "Sentence 2: Change the colon after <i>dramatically</i> to a comma AND change <i>could</i> to <i>can</i>.", "Sentence 3: Delete the comma after <i>Rochester</i> AND change the semicolon after <i>quickly</i> to a comma.", "Sentence 4: Change <i>its</i> to <i>their</i> AND change the comma after <i>own</i> to a semicolon."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the pair of revisions needed to correct the errors in the paragraph, both of which appear in sentence 4.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “1825” is needed to separate the introductory clause “Before the Erie Canal opened in 1825” from the main clause. The verb “took” is correctly in the past tense, matching “cost” and the rest of the paragraph.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The colon after “dramatically” correctly follows a complete sentence and introduces a second sentence that explains it; a comma there would create a run-on. The verb “could” is correct in a paragraph about the 1820s.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “Rochester” is needed to close the nonrestrictive phrase “such as Syracuse and Rochester,” which is already set off by a comma after “route.” The semicolon after “quickly” correctly joins two complete sentences; the word “meanwhile” is not a conjunction, so a comma there would create a run-on.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> The waterways belonged to the “other states,” a plural noun, so the possessive must be the plural “their” rather than “its.” The word “however” begins a second complete sentence (“the spread of railroads in the 1840s soon made most of them unprofitable”), and because “however” is not a conjunction, a semicolon rather than a comma is needed after “own.”</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-04", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-04", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p><blockquote>(1) Last spring, the students in Ms. Okafor’s science classes turned an empty lot behind the school into a vegetable garden. (2) The owners of a neighborhood hardware store, who had read about the project in a newsletter, was glad to donate supplies such as: lumber for the raised beds, bags of soil, and trays of seedlings. (3) By June the beds were full of lettuce, radishes, and herbs; the tomatoes, however, did not ripen until late August. (4) The students learned one lesson quickly: a garden that is not watered every day will not survive a New York summer.</blockquote>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Insert a comma after <i>classes</i> AND change <i>turned</i> to <i>turn</i>.", "Sentence 2: Change <i>was</i> to <i>were</i> AND delete the colon after <i>such as</i>.", "Sentence 3: Change the semicolon after <i>herbs</i> to a comma AND delete the comma after <i>tomatoes</i>.", "Sentence 4: Change the colon after <i>quickly</i> to a semicolon AND change <i>is</i> to <i>was</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the pair of revisions needed to correct the errors in the paragraph, both of which appear in sentence 2.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> A comma after “classes” would wrongly separate the subject “students” from its verb “turned.” The verb “turned” is correctly in the past tense for an event that happened last spring.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> The subject of the sentence is the plural noun “owners,” not the singular nouns “store,” “project,” or “newsletter” that come between it and the verb, so the verb must be the plural “were.” A colon must follow a complete sentence, but the words before this colon end with “such as” and are not complete; the phrase “such as” introduces the list by itself, so the colon should be deleted.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The semicolon after “herbs” correctly joins two complete sentences. Here “however” interrupts the second sentence rather than beginning it, so it is correctly set off by a pair of commas; a comma after “herbs” would create a run-on, and deleting the comma after “tomatoes” would leave “however” set off on only one side.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The colon after “quickly” correctly follows a complete sentence and introduces the lesson that the sentence promises. The present-tense “is” is correct because the lesson is a general truth, and it matches the verb “will not survive”; “was” would not.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-05", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-05", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p><blockquote>(1) Every autumn, millions of monarch butterflies leave the northern United States and Canada, their destination is a small group of mountain forests in central Mexico, where it spends the winter clustered on fir trees. (2) The trip can cover nearly 3,000 miles; no single butterfly, however, has ever made it before. (3) Scientists believe that the insects rely on two guides: the position of the sun and the magnetic field of Earth. (4) The generation that flies south lives as long as eight months, but each of the generations that return north in the spring survives for only a few weeks.</blockquote>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Change the comma after <i>Canada</i> to a semicolon AND change <i>it spends</i> to <i>they spend</i>.", "Sentence 2: Change the semicolon after <i>miles</i> to a comma AND delete the comma after <i>however</i>.", "Sentence 3: Delete the colon after <i>guides</i> AND change <i>rely</i> to <i>relies</i>.", "Sentence 4: Delete the comma after <i>months</i> AND change <i>survives</i> to <i>survive</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the pair of revisions needed to correct the errors in the paragraph, both of which appear in sentence 1.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> The words before and after the comma following “Canada” are both complete sentences (“millions of monarch butterflies leave the northern United States and Canada” and “their destination is a small group of mountain forests in central Mexico”), so they must be joined by a semicolon rather than a comma. In addition, the pronoun refers to the plural “butterflies,” so “it spends” must be the plural “they spend.”</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The semicolon after “miles” correctly joins two complete sentences; a comma there would create a run-on. Here “however” interrupts the second sentence, so it is correctly set off by a comma on each side.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The colon after “guides” correctly follows a complete sentence and introduces the two guides it mentions. The plural verb “rely” agrees with the plural subject “insects.”</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “months” is needed before the conjunction “but,” which joins two complete sentences. The singular verb “survives” is correct because its subject is the singular pronoun “each,” not the plural noun “generations”; the plural verb “return” belongs to “generations” and is also correct.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-06", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-06", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>According to the museum’s guide, the materials that the sculptor used most often in her early work, which she completed before 1950, include: scrap steel, driftwood, and broken glass.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change the comma after <i>guide</i> to a colon.", "Change <i>include</i> to <i>includes</i>.", "Change the comma after <i>1950</i> to a semicolon.", "Delete the colon after <i>include</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks which edit corrects the sentence, which misuses a colon.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “guide” correctly sets off the introductory phrase “According to the museum’s guide.” That phrase is not a complete sentence, so a colon cannot follow it.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The plural verb “include” agrees with the plural subject “materials.” The singular nouns “work” and “1950” that come just before the verb are not its subject.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “1950” closes the nonrestrictive clause “which she completed before 1950.” A semicolon there would separate the subject “materials” from its verb “include.”</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> A colon must follow a complete sentence. The words before this colon end with the verb “include,” which still needs the list to complete its meaning, so no punctuation belongs between “include” and “scrap steel, driftwood, and broken glass.”</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-07", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-07", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>The city council had planned to close the Ninth Street pool at the end of the summer, however, hundreds of residents signed a petition, and the members voted to keep it open.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Delete the comma after <i>however</i>.", "Change the comma after <i>summer</i> to a semicolon.", "Change the comma after <i>petition</i> to a semicolon.", "Change <i>had planned</i> to <i>plans</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks which edit corrects the sentence, which joins two complete sentences with only a comma and the word <i>however</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “however” is needed to set off the transition from the sentence it introduces. Deleting it would not repair the run-on at “summer.”</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> “The city council had planned to close the Ninth Street pool at the end of the summer” is a complete sentence, and so are the words that begin with “however.” Because “however” is not a conjunction, a semicolon is required before it, with a comma after it.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “petition” correctly comes before the conjunction “and,” which joins two complete sentences. Changing it would leave the run-on at “summer” uncorrected.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The past perfect “had planned” is correct because the council made its plan before the residents “signed” and the members “voted.” The present-tense “plans” would not fit these past-tense verbs.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-08", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-08", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>The Hubble Space Telescope sent back blurry images at first; because its main mirror had been ground to a slightly incorrect shape, a flaw that astronauts corrected in 1993.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change the comma after <i>shape</i> to a semicolon.", "Change the semicolon after <i>first</i> to a colon.", "Delete the semicolon after <i>first</i>.", "Change <i>had been</i> to <i>has been</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks which edit corrects the sentence, which uses a semicolon to join a complete sentence to a group of words that cannot stand alone.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The comma after “shape” correctly sets off the phrase “a flaw that astronauts corrected in 1993,” which renames the mirror’s incorrect shape. That phrase is not a complete sentence, so a semicolon cannot come before it.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> A colon introduces a list, an example, or a sentence that explains the one before it. The words after “first” already begin with “because,” which attaches the reason directly to “sent back blurry images at first,” so a colon is no better than the semicolon.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> A semicolon must have a complete sentence on each side. The words that begin with “because” are a dependent clause, not a sentence, so they should follow “at first” with no punctuation: “sent back blurry images at first because its main mirror had been ground to a slightly incorrect shape.”</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The past perfect “had been ground” is correct because the mirror was shaped before the telescope “sent back” its images. The present perfect “has been” would not fit a sentence about the early 1990s.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-09", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-09", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Which sentence uses a colon correctly?</p>", "opts": ["For the overnight hike, each student should bring: a water bottle, a rain jacket, and a pair of sturdy shoes.", "The coach gave her players one final instruction before the match: pass the ball before the defense can close in.", "The new wing of the museum has several attractions that appeal to children, such as: a planetarium, a fossil lab, and a small theater.", "The three sites that the committee considered for the mural were: the gym wall, the front lobby, and the library entrance."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence uses a colon correctly. A colon must come after words that could stand alone as a complete sentence.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The words before the colon end with the verb “bring,” which needs the list to complete its meaning. No punctuation belongs between “bring” and the items.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> “The coach gave her players one final instruction before the match” is a complete sentence, and the words after the colon state the instruction that it promises.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The phrase “such as” already introduces the examples, and the words that end with “such as” are not a complete sentence. The colon should be deleted.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The words before the colon end with the verb “were,” which needs the list to complete its meaning. No punctuation belongs between “were” and the items.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:punct-10", "form": "Extra", "n": "punct-10", "tag": "punct", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Which sentence uses semicolons correctly?</p>", "opts": ["Next month the choir will perform in three cities; Albany, Hartford, and Providence.", "Many students at the school walk to class each morning; while others take the bus or the subway.", "Last season the team played in Buffalo, New York; Erie, Pennsylvania; and Akron, Ohio.", "The library’s oldest book; a hand-copied atlas from 1640, is kept in a locked glass case."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks which sentence uses semicolons correctly. A semicolon either joins two complete sentences or separates the items of a list when the items themselves contain commas.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The words after the semicolon, “Albany, Hartford, and Providence,” are a list, not a complete sentence. A colon is needed to introduce the list.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The words “while others take the bus or the subway” are a dependent clause and cannot stand alone as a sentence. A comma, not a semicolon, should follow “morning.”</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> Each item in the list is a city and its state, and each already contains a comma. Semicolons between the items show where one place ends and the next begins.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The phrase “a hand-copied atlas from 1640” renames “book” and must be set off by a pair of commas. The semicolon separates the subject “book” from its verb “is kept.”</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-01", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-01", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, it was the longest suspension bridge in the world. (2) Its chief engineer, Washington Roebling, had fallen ill years earlier, so his wife, Emily, carries his instructions to the workers and learned enough engineering to answer their questions herself. (3) Many New Yorkers doubted that the bridge was safe until a showman led twenty-one elephants across it the following year. (4) Today tens of thousands of people cross the bridge each day, and it remains one of the most recognizable landmarks in the city.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct an inappropriate shift in verb tense?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence with an inappropriate shift in verb tense. The paragraph describes the building of the bridge in the past tense, so the present-tense verb <i>carries</i> in sentence 2 should be <i>carried</i>, matching <i>learned</i> in the same sentence.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 1 keeps both verbs, <i>opened</i> and <i>was</i>, in the past tense.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 2 shifts to the present tense with <i>carries</i>. Emily Roebling’s work took place during construction, so the verb should be <i>carried</i>. The past perfect <i>had fallen</i> is not the error; it correctly marks an event that came before her work.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 3 uses the past-tense verbs <i>doubted</i>, <i>was</i>, and <i>led</i> for events of the 1880s.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 4 changes to the present tense (<i>cross</i>, <i>remains</i>), but the word <i>Today</i> signals that the sentence describes the present, so the change is appropriate.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-02", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-02", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Scientists have long known that honeybees communicate through movement. (2) In the 1940s, the Austrian researcher Karl von Frisch discovered that a bee returning to the hive performs a “waggle dance” that tells other bees where food is. (3) He had spent decades watching marked bees before he was confident enough to publish his conclusions. (4) Many biologists rejected his claims at first, but later experiments confirm them, and in 1973 he shared a Nobel Prize for his work.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct an inappropriate shift in verb tense?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence with an inappropriate shift in verb tense. Sentence 4 narrates past events with <i>rejected</i> and <i>shared</i>, so the present-tense verb <i>confirm</i> between them should be <i>confirmed</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 1 uses the present perfect <i>have long known</i> for knowledge that continues today and the present-tense <i>communicate</i> for a general fact about bees.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 2 uses the past-tense <i>discovered</i> for the event and the present-tense <i>performs</i>, <i>tells</i>, and <i>is</i> for what bees always do. A general truth stays in the present tense even when it was discovered in the past.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 3 uses the past perfect <i>had spent</i> for the years of observation that came before he <i>was</i> confident enough to publish.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 4 shifts to the present tense with <i>confirm</i>. The experiments took place after the first rejection and before the 1973 prize, so the verb should be <i>confirmed</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-03", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-03", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Last spring, the seventh-grade science classes at Hudson Middle School planted a vegetable garden behind the gym, and each student chooses one crop to tend. (2) The students had tested the soil in March, so they knew that it needed more nitrogen before anything would grow. (3) In June, the garden produced enough lettuce and radishes to supply the cafeteria’s salad bar for two weeks. (4) The classes will expand the garden next year, and the school plans to add a small greenhouse as well.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct an inappropriate shift in verb tense?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence with an inappropriate shift in verb tense. Sentence 1 describes what happened last spring with <i>planted</i>, so the present-tense verb <i>chooses</i> should be <i>chose</i>.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 1 shifts from the past-tense <i>planted</i> to the present-tense <i>chooses</i>. Both actions happened last spring, so the verb should be <i>chose</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 2 uses the past perfect <i>had tested</i> because the soil test in March came before the planting, and <i>knew</i>, <i>needed</i>, and <i>would grow</i> all fit the past time frame.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 3 uses the past-tense <i>produced</i> for what the garden did by June.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 4 uses the future <i>will expand</i> and the present <i>plans</i>, but the words <i>next year</i> show that the sentence describes a plan that has not yet happened, so these tenses are appropriate.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-04", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-04", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Before the Erie Canal opened in 1825, moving a ton of grain from Buffalo to New York City took about three weeks and cost roughly one hundred dollars. (2) Critics had mocked the project as “Clinton’s Ditch,” after Governor DeWitt Clinton, who championed it. (3) Once boats began traveling the canal, however, the cost of shipping drops by about ninety percent, and towns along the route grew rapidly. (4) Although freight now moves mostly by rail and highway, the canal still carries pleasure boats, and historians regard it as one of the most important public works in American history.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct an inappropriate shift in verb tense?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence with an inappropriate shift in verb tense. Sentence 3 describes the 1820s with <i>began</i> and <i>grew</i>, so the present-tense verb <i>drops</i> should be <i>dropped</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 1 uses the past-tense verbs <i>opened</i>, <i>took</i>, and <i>cost</i>. (<i>Cost</i> has the same form in the past and the present; here it is past.)</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 2 uses the past perfect <i>had mocked</i> because the mockery came before the canal opened, and the past-tense <i>championed</i>.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 3 shifts to the present tense with <i>drops</i>. The fall in shipping costs happened when the boats <i>began</i> traveling and the towns <i>grew</i>, so the verb should be <i>dropped</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 4 changes to the present tense (<i>moves</i>, <i>carries</i>, <i>regard</i>), but the words <i>now</i> and <i>still</i> signal that the sentence describes the present, so the change is appropriate.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-05", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-05", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Sound travels as vibrations, which is why a guitar string must move in order to be heard. (2) In the 1930s, musicians in large dance bands complained that their acoustic guitars could not compete with the horns and drums. (3) Inventors had experimented with microphones attached to the instrument, but these picked up unwanted noise. (4) The solution came when engineers built a magnetic pickup that sensed the motion of the steel strings directly and sends it to an amplifier as an electrical signal.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct an inappropriate shift in verb tense?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 3, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence with an inappropriate shift in verb tense. In sentence 4 the pickup that engineers <i>built</i> did two things, and the first is in the past tense (<i>sensed</i>), so the second verb, <i>sends</i>, should be <i>sent</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 1 is in the present tense (<i>travels</i>, <i>is</i>, <i>must move</i>) because it states a scientific fact that is always true.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 2 uses the past-tense <i>complained</i> and <i>could not compete</i> for the 1930s.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 3 uses the past perfect <i>had experimented</i> for attempts that came before the solution in sentence 4, and the past-tense <i>picked up</i>.</p><p><b>D. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 4 shifts to the present tense with <i>sends</i>. The verbs <i>sensed</i> and <i>sends</i> share the subject <i>that</i> (the pickup) and are joined by <i>and</i>, so they must match: <i>sensed … and sent</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-06", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-06", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Every November, the New York City Marathon draws more than 50,000 runners, who cover 26.2 miles through all five boroughs. (2) The race begins on Staten Island, crosses the Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge into Brooklyn, and wound north through Queens, Manhattan, and the Bronx before it ends in Central Park. (3) The first race, held in 1970, was far smaller: only 127 runners entered, and they ran the whole distance inside the park. (4) Organizers moved the course onto city streets in 1976, and the event has grown nearly every year since.</blockquote><p>Which sentence should be revised to correct an inappropriate shift in verb tense?</p>", "opts": ["sentence 1", "sentence 2", "sentence 3", "sentence 4"], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the sentence with an inappropriate shift in verb tense. Sentence 2 describes the route the race follows every year in the present tense (<i>begins</i>, <i>crosses</i>, <i>ends</i>), so the past-tense verb <i>wound</i> should be <i>winds</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 1 uses the present-tense <i>draws</i> and <i>cover</i> for something that happens every November.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> Sentence 2 shifts to the past tense with <i>wound</i> in the middle of a series of present-tense verbs. The sentence should read <i>begins … crosses … and winds</i>.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 3 changes to the past tense (<i>was</i>, <i>entered</i>, <i>ran</i>), but it describes the first race, in 1970, so the change is appropriate.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> Sentence 4 uses the past-tense <i>moved</i> for an event in 1976 and the present perfect <i>has grown</i> with <i>since</i> for growth that began then and continues today.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-07", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-07", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>When the explorers finally reached the summit, they realize that the storm they had seen from the valley was moving toward them faster than their guide had predicted.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>realize</i> to <i>realized</i>.", "Change <i>had seen</i> to <i>see</i>.", "Change <i>was moving</i> to <i>moves</i>.", "Change <i>had predicted</i> to <i>predicts</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 0, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects the sentence. The sentence tells about a past event (<i>reached</i>), so the present-tense verb <i>realize</i> is an inappropriate shift and should be <i>realized</i>.</p><p><b>A. CORRECT.</b> The explorers <i>reached</i> the summit in the past, and what they understood at that moment must also be in the past: <i>they realized</i>.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The past perfect <i>had seen</i> is correct because the explorers saw the storm from the valley before they reached the summit.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The past progressive <i>was moving</i> is correct because the storm was in motion at the past moment the sentence describes.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The past perfect <i>had predicted</i> is correct because the guide made the prediction earlier than the events at the summit.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-08", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-08", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this sentence.</p><blockquote>Although the museum closed its original building in 2019, it now occupies a renovated warehouse near the river, where visitors examined fossils that scientists collected more than a century ago.</blockquote><p>Which edit should be made to correct this sentence?</p>", "opts": ["Change <i>closed</i> to <i>closes</i>.", "Change <i>occupies</i> to <i>occupied</i>.", "Change <i>examined</i> to <i>examine</i>.", "Change <i>collected</i> to <i>collect</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the edit that corrects the sentence. The main clause describes the museum as it is <i>now</i> (<i>occupies</i>), so what visitors do there should also be in the present tense: <i>examine</i>, not <i>examined</i>.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The past-tense <i>closed</i> is correct because the closing happened in 2019.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The present-tense <i>occupies</i> is correct because the word <i>now</i> shows that the sentence describes the museum’s current home.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> Visitors look at the fossils in the building the museum <i>now occupies</i>, so the verb must be the present-tense <i>examine</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The past-tense <i>collected</i> is correct because the scientists gathered the fossils <i>more than a century ago</i>.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-09", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-09", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) When New York’s first subway line opened on October 27, 1904, more than 100,000 people paid a nickel each to ride it. (2) The line ran from City Hall to 145th Street; the trip, which had taken far longer by streetcar, lasted only about twenty-six minutes. (3) City officials had expected large crowds, however, they are astonished when riders waited in lines that stretched for blocks. (4) Today the system has 472 stations, and it carries millions of passengers every weekday.</blockquote><p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Change <i>opened</i> to <i>opens</i> AND delete the comma after <i>1904</i>.", "Sentence 2: Change the semicolon to a comma AND change <i>had taken</i> to <i>takes</i>.", "Sentence 3: Change the comma after <i>crowds</i> to a semicolon AND change <i>are</i> to <i>were</i>.", "Sentence 4: Change <i>has</i> to <i>had</i> AND delete the comma after <i>stations</i>."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 2, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the pair of revisions that corrects the paragraph. Sentence 3 joins two complete sentences with only a comma before <i>however</i>, and it shifts to the present tense with <i>are</i> in a past-tense account.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The past-tense <i>opened</i> matches the date 1904, and the comma after <i>1904</i> correctly ends the introductory clause.</p><p><b>B. Incorrect.</b> The semicolon correctly joins two complete sentences, and changing it to a comma would create a run-on. The past perfect <i>had taken</i> correctly refers to streetcar trips before the subway existed.</p><p><b>C. CORRECT.</b> <i>City officials had expected large crowds</i> and <i>they were astonished …</i> are both complete sentences, so a semicolon is needed before <i>however</i>. The officials’ reaction happened in 1904, so <i>are</i> must be <i>were</i>, matching <i>waited</i> and <i>stretched</i>.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The word <i>Today</i> shows that sentence 4 describes the present, so <i>has</i> is correct, and the comma before <i>and</i> correctly separates two complete sentences.</p>"}, {"id": "gg:tense-10", "form": "Extra", "n": "tense-10", "tag": "tense", "pid": null, "html": "<p>Read this paragraph.</p><blockquote>(1) Each autumn, millions of monarch butterflies leave the northern United States and Canada; they fly as far as 3,000 miles to reach central Mexico. (2) Scientists did not know where the insects spent the winter until 1975, when a research team finally locates the colonies in: a few high mountain forests west of Mexico City. (3) The team had relied on thousands of volunteers, who tagged butterflies and reported where the tags turned up. (4) Because the forests are now threatened by logging, conservation groups are working to protect them: the monarchs’ survival depends on these small patches of woodland.</blockquote><p>Which pair of revisions need to be made in this paragraph?</p>", "opts": ["Sentence 1: Change <i>leave</i> to <i>left</i> AND change the semicolon to a comma.", "Sentence 2: Change <i>locates</i> to <i>located</i> AND delete the colon after <i>in</i>.", "Sentence 3: Change <i>had relied</i> to <i>relies</i> AND delete the comma after <i>volunteers</i>.", "Sentence 4: Change <i>are working</i> to <i>worked</i> AND change the colon to a comma."], "letters": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "a": 1, "exp": "<p>The question asks for the pair of revisions that corrects the paragraph. Sentence 2 shifts to the present tense with <i>locates</i> for an event in 1975, and it places a colon after the preposition <i>in</i>, where no punctuation belongs.</p><p><b>A. Incorrect.</b> The present-tense <i>leave</i> is correct for something that happens <i>each autumn</i>, and the semicolon correctly joins two complete sentences; a comma there would create a run-on.</p><p><b>B. CORRECT.</b> The discovery happened in 1975, so the verb must be <i>located</i>, matching <i>did not know</i>. A colon must follow a complete sentence, and <i>a research team finally located the colonies in</i> is not complete, so the colon should be deleted.</p><p><b>C. Incorrect.</b> The past perfect <i>had relied</i> correctly describes help that came before the discovery, and the comma correctly sets off the clause beginning with <i>who</i>, which adds extra information about the volunteers.</p><p><b>D. Incorrect.</b> The word <i>now</i> shows that sentence 4 describes the present, so <i>are working</i> is correct. The colon follows a complete sentence and introduces an explanation; a comma there would create a run-on.</p>"}];
